-- §5  What each filing says about couplings, and whether it says anything at all.
# One row per corpus filing: how many couplings it states, and what its coupling search reported
# if it states none. Those are two different facts and an empty table carries only the first.
#
# Corpus only, and a report
#   That is the whole reason the distinction exists. "No filing asserts independence" is a fact
#   about the evidence; a stipulation that asserts it makes the finding a lie.
#   `assets/fixtures/every-absence.xml` files exactly that, on purpose, to prove the branch works,
#   and it must not be counted here. Rules run on fixtures; reports about the world do not. See
#   `assets/fixtures/README.md`.
#
# The scope is a slot
#   It is a relation passed into a slot rather than a `WHERE` on a column, so the document set
#   this report ranges over is something somebody named on purpose, and a caller who forgets to
#   name one does not compose at all. `matrices.sql` fills the same slot with every filing, for a
#   reason its own section argues.
-- entries/coupling_presence.sqlc over scope/corpus.sqlc.
SELECT p.filing            AS "filing!",
       p.couplings_filed   AS "n!",
       p.search_answer::text AS why
FROM (
    :compose(entries/coupling_presence.sqlc, @scope = scope/corpus.sqlc)
) p
ORDER BY p.filing
